  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=-2$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x} & \\text{\u00a0for\u00a0}\\,x<-2 \\\\ {e}^{2+x}+3 & \\text{\u00a0for\u00a0}\\,x\\ge -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-6(-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x\\ge -2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{2+x}+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}{e}^{2+x}+3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2+(-2)}+3 \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+3 \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$",
